<!--
    Kuna #1157 (secondary): decompiling python314.dll at 0x180099df0
    panicked in TransformManager::apply (index -2 into a two-lane piece array).
    Upstream Ghidra fixed the same line as GP-7434 ("Fix for LaneDivide
    terminator SUBPIECE trace", NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra de40b0bf5dc6).

    Stage: P3 dataflow, laned-register split (ActionLaneDivide driving
    LaneDivide::traceForward).  A SUBPIECE too narrow for a lane, read from a
    varnode that holds only the upper lanes of the split register, is kept as a
    terminating SUBPIECE of the lane it starts in.  That lane was found from
    the SUBPIECE's byte offset alone, which counts from the varnode, not from
    the whole register, so it was off by the varnode's skipped lanes.

    Authored x86-64 (GNU as), no option: a strict correctness fix.
    f_hi:  movups xmm6,[rdi]; movd eax,xmm6; psrldq a copy by 8; movq rcx,xmm0.
           rcx (lanes 2..3 of the 4x4-byte split) is loop-carried and its low
           byte is read (sub r8b,cl): lane index 0 - 2 skipped lanes = -2, the
           panic.  The byte is a0[2]'s.
    f_mid: the same with psrldq 4 (rcx = lanes 1..2) and the byte at rcx+4
           (shr r9,32; sub r8b,r9b): the old lane was 1 (a0[1]) instead of 2
           (a0[2]), a silent wrong read.
    f_hi at 0x100000, f_mid at 0x100080.

    Before the fix: f_hi's decompilation fails (assert no_failed_decompile)
    and f_mid subtracts a0[1]'s byte, so neither function reads a0[2] or
    a0[6] (asserts lane2_load, lane2_reload).  After: both read the byte of
    lane 2, from the first load and from the reload.
-->
